00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:28.460
I'm gonna draw four.

00:09:28.460 --> 00:09:29.720
Okay, nothing special there.

00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:33.900
If you scroll down, what you're
seeing here is a model is trying

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:36.580
to guess what digit I wrote.

00:09:36.580 --> 00:09:40.420
And what it's showing me with
these bars is the probability,

00:09:40.420 --> 00:09:43.940
according to the model that what
I drew was a zero,

00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:45.920
a one, a two, a three, a four,

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:47.940
five, six, seven, eight, or
nine.

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:52.240
And what did you notice with
this four?

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:55.240
What do you notice?

00:09:57.180 --> 00:09:58.940
Yes, and remind us of your name?

00:09:58.940 --> 00:09:59.880
Yeah.

00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:00.820
Allison.
Allison.

00:10:00.820 --> 00:10:02.180
Thinks it's an eight.

00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:03.700
It thinks it's an eight.

00:10:03.700 --> 00:10:04.600
Yeah, exactly.

00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:06.880
The most likely outcome
according to the model was that

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:08.100
this was an eight.

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:11.220
It thinks, OK, maybe it could be
a four or five, but it seemed

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:14.160
like greater than a 50% chance
that it's an 8.

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:18.680
What if I draw my 4 this way?

00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:22.080
It still is sort of split
between a 4 and an 8, but now it

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:24.520
starts to think it's a 9.

00:10:24.520 --> 00:10:27.840
What if I draw a 9 like that?

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:30.080
It looks kind of like the 4 that
I just drew,

00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:32.620
but it's pretty confident that
this is a 9.

00:10:32.620 --> 00:10:34.820
What if I draw a 1 there?

00:10:34.820 --> 00:10:35.940
It thinks it's a 1.

00:10:35.940 --> 00:10:38.560
What if I draw the 1 over here?
Now it starts to think a little

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:40.280
bit that it's a seven.

00:10:40.280 --> 00:10:42.680
What if I draw a zero here?
Well, it's pretty confident that

00:10:42.680 --> 00:10:43.740
it's a two.

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:46.940
So what we're seeing is that
sometimes this model works

00:10:46.940 --> 00:10:50.620
well, and sometimes it doesn't
work well.

00:10:50.620 --> 00:10:52.760
And I encourage you to play
around with this and see if you

00:10:52.760 --> 00:10:55.040
can fool it in weird ways.

00:10:55.040 --> 00:10:56.080
Like there it thinks it's a
nine.

00:10:56.080 --> 00:11:01.260
It thinks this zero is a nine?

00:11:01.260 --> 00:11:01.960
Yeah,

00:11:01.960 --> 00:11:02.860
everyone else have your name?

00:11:02.860 --> 00:11:03.580
Gavin?

00:11:03.580 --> 00:11:05.920
That's where if you were going
to complete the nine, and that's

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:07.340
where the hoop would be.

00:11:07.340 --> 00:11:09.040
Exactly.
If we were to draw a nine,

00:11:09.140 --> 00:11:12.700
that's what it would look like.
But maybe if I start up here,

00:11:12.700 --> 00:11:16.500
I can get it to think it's an
eight as well.

00:11:16.500 --> 00:11:21.060
So this example of handwritten
digit classification

00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:25.180
is actually one of the first
examples of a machine learning

00:11:25.180 --> 00:11:29.480
system that was employed by
society at large.

00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:35.400
And it was actually developed by
USPS in the late 1990s to

00:11:35.400 --> 00:11:38.400
transcribe the addresses people
wrote on letters.

