them the address and they type

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:51.380
it in.
But before you would write a

00:11:51.380 --> 00:11:54.420
letter and USPS developed this,
or they didn't develop it, but

00:11:54.420 --> 00:11:56.000
they used this technology

00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:58.440
to figure out who to send their
packages to.

00:11:58.440 --> 00:12:01.720
And this is an example of a
machine learning system.

00:12:01.720 --> 00:12:02.140
Now,

00:12:02.140 --> 00:12:05.820
what I want to illustrate here
is what makes this a machine

00:12:05.820 --> 00:12:07.300
learning system.

00:12:07.300 --> 00:12:11.020
If you had to develop a system
like this on your own to figure

00:12:11.020 --> 00:12:13.280
out what digit is written down,

00:12:13.280 --> 00:12:19.200
one way you could do this is to
come up with a massive set of if

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:20.200
statements.

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:21.400
You could say things like,

00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:23.160
if there's a circle,

00:12:23.160 --> 00:12:24.640
well, then it could be an 8,

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:26.260
but it could be a 0.

00:12:26.260 --> 00:12:28.320
Or maybe it's a 2 with the loop
at the bottom.

00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:29.700
Or maybe it's a 9.

00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:30.760
If there's a stick,

00:12:30.760 --> 00:12:34.940
well, then maybe it's a 1.
maybe it's a seven,

00:12:34.940 --> 00:12:37.260
you know, maybe it's a nine.
You could come up with a bunch

00:12:37.260 --> 00:12:39.420
of if statements like that.
But what's the problem with that

00:12:39.420 --> 00:12:40.420
approach?

00:12:43.340 --> 00:12:46.140
What's the problem with just
writing hundreds of if

00:12:46.140 --> 00:12:48.000
statements? Yes, and remind us
of your name?

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:48.400
Ammar.

00:12:48.400 --> 00:12:49.800
I think it's just like tedious.

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:51.520
Yeah, it's tedious.
And here there are only 10

00:12:51.520 --> 00:12:54.420
possible categories or classes,

00:12:54.420 --> 00:12:55.700
zero through nine.

00:12:55.700 --> 00:12:59.000
But what if we were trying to
figure out what letter people

00:12:59.000 --> 00:12:59.920
wrote?

00:12:59.920 --> 00:13:02.600
Then there are 26 options,

00:13:02.600 --> 00:13:05.000
why stop at letters and numbers?
What if we were trying to figure

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:10.060
out what animal we took a
picture of? How would you write

00:13:10.060 --> 00:13:13.840
if statements that accurately
capture all of that

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:14.840
sophistication?

00:13:15.420 --> 00:13:17.760
So that approach that I just
described of writing a bunch of

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:18.980
if statements,

00:13:18.980 --> 00:13:20.900
that is not the machine learning
approach.

00:13:20.900 --> 00:13:23.860
OK? Instead, the machine
learning approach

00:13:23.860 --> 00:13:28.240
is to start with a data set,

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:29.240
OK?

00:13:29.300 --> 00:13:32.540
And what you do is you start
with a data set.

00:13:32.540 --> 00:13:33.940
And in this example,

00:13:33.940 --> 00:13:36.760
you start with a data set that
is labeled,

00:13:36.760 --> 00:13:40.260
or in other words, a data set in
which you know the right answers

00:13:40.260 --> 00:13:41.200
already.

00:13:41.200 --> 00:13:43.340
That's what a label is.

00:13:43.340 --> 00:13:46.680
So to train this handwritten
digit classifier,

00:13:46.680 --> 00:13:48.620
what we did, or what they did,

00:13:48.620 --> 00:13:53.280
is it took lots of images of
zeros that people wrote and

00:13:53.280 --> 00:13:55.480
wrote down, okay, these are all
zeros,

00:13:55.480 --> 00:13:58.920
and then took lots of pictures
of ones that people wrote and

00:13:58.920 --> 00:14:01.160
said, okay, these are pictures
of ones, and these are pictures

00:14:01.160 --> 00:14:03.700
of twos, and these are pictures
of threes.

00:14:03.700 --> 00:14:07.820
And so what we do is we show the
system lots of labeled examples

00:14:07.820 --> 00:14:11.520
of what each category is
supposed to look like, and the

00:14:11.520 --> 00:14:15.440
idea is that the system
automatically learns how to

00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:19.180
distinguish ones from twos from
threes and fours.

00:14:19.180 --> 00:14:20.360
Okay, And as we see,

00:14:20.360 --> 00:14:23.820
it doesn't necessarily do this
perfectly,

00:14:23.820 --> 00:14:26.720
but that is the machine learning
approach.

00:14:26.720 --> 00:14:30.260
And by developing better machine
learning models, we build

00:14:30.260 --> 00:14:33.840
systems that are more accurately
able to do things like classify

00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:35.340
handwritten digits.

00:14:35.660 --> 00:14:38.380
But that's the core idea I
wanted to impart here.

00:14:38.380 --> 00:14:44.260
The machine learning approach to
solving

00:14:44.260 --> 00:14:47.860
problems This is about
automatically

00:14:47.860 --> 00:14:53.860
recognizing patterns in

00:14:53.860 --> 00:14:54.860
data.

00:15:02.360 --> 00:15:05.020
And this definition of machine
learning,

00:15:05.020 --> 00:15:07.580
automatically recognizing
patterns in data,

00:15:07.820 --> 00:15:12.180
applies to other sort of systems
that you've heard about in

00:15:12.180 --> 00:15:14.840
popular media that involve
machine learning and artificial

00:15:14.840 --> 00:15:15.840
intelligence.

00:15:16.060 --> 00:15:16.880
ChatGPT,

00:15:16.880 --> 00:15:19.820
I think you're all pretty aware
that what it does

00:15:19.820 --> 00:15:23.840
is it tries to regurgitate
things that sound similar to

00:15:23.840 --> 00:15:28.200
what it saw in its training
data, which is why it's pretty

00:15:28.200 --> 00:15:31.900
obvious when someone has just
copy-pasted output from Claude.

00:15:31.900 --> 00:15:34.720
And it's because most of the
ChatGPT and Claude and Gemini

00:15:34.720 --> 00:15:36.840
outputs sound very similar
because they're trying to mimic

00:15:36.840 --> 00:15:38.620
patterns that they've already
seen.

00:15:38.620 --> 00:15:40.300
And to create these systems,

00:15:40.300 --> 00:15:43.940
they used trillions of gigabytes
of data,

00:15:43.940 --> 00:15:46.940
and that's why they seem to know
about so many different things.

00:15:46.940 --> 00:15:48.000
Now, it's not just that,

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:48.640
right?

00:15:48.640 --> 00:15:51.200
Tools like Chachi Bhati also
have the ability to look things

00:15:51.200 --> 00:15:52.320
up on the internet,

00:15:52.320 --> 00:15:54.480
so if it didn't already know
about something, it can learn

00:15:54.480 --> 00:15:55.540
about it.

00:15:55.540 --> 00:15:58.020
But that's the crux of it.

00:15:58.020 --> 00:16:00.920
Out of curiosity, how many
people here knew that the person

00:16:00.920 --> 00:16:05.420
that Claude is named after went
to Michigan?

00:16:05.620 --> 00:16:09.780
So Claude is named after a
famous mathematician computer

00:16:09.780 --> 00:16:13.530
scientist from the 1930s named
Claude Shannon.

00:16:13.760 --> 00:16:14.900
And he's like,

00:16:14.900 --> 00:16:16.940
he's not super like,

00:16:16.940 --> 00:16:19.840
people say he's like Einstein or
Newton level genius but just

00:16:19.840 --> 00:16:23.340
doesn't have the PR that those
people had.

00:16:23.340 --> 00:16:26.060
But there's actually a statue of
him on North Campus in front of

00:16:26.060 --> 00:16:27.460
the expo.
You should go look at it and

00:16:27.460 --> 00:16:28.780
it'll say Claude Shannon.

00:16:28.780 --> 00:16:31.020
And if you search him up, you'll
see that he developed a lot of

00:16:31.020 --> 00:16:33.960
the foundational theories used
in machine learning and AI.

00:16:33.960 --> 00:16:35.840
And that's who Anthropic named
the system after.

00:16:35.840 --> 00:16:38.060
And he went to Michigan for his
master's and PhD.

00:16:38.060 --> 00:16:39.060
Okay,

00:16:40.180 --> 00:16:42.200
so we're comfortable with my
definition of machine learning,

00:16:42.200 --> 00:16:44.680
automatically recognizing
patterns in data.

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:46.640
Okay,

00:16:46.640 --> 00:16:50.600
so what I want to give us is a
broad understanding of the types

00:16:50.600 --> 00:16:53.520
of problems one can solve using
machine learning.

00:16:53.520 --> 00:16:55.640
And maybe this is a slightly
outdated approach because

00:16:55.640 --> 00:16:59.540
something like ChatGPT doesn't
directly fit into this taxonomy,

00:16:59.540 --> 00:17:01.360
but I'll talk about that in just
a little bit.

00:17:01.360 --> 00:17:04.620
But for the most part, our focus
is going to be on a smaller

00:17:04.620 --> 00:17:07.120
sliver of machine learning, and
we will start from there and

00:17:07.120 --> 00:17:09.040
build your foundation from
there.

00:17:09.040 --> 00:17:09.900
OK.

00:17:09.900 --> 00:17:10.920
For us,

00:17:10.920 --> 00:17:15.800
I want to look here into these
two broad categories

00:17:15.800 --> 00:17:18.920
of supervised learning and
unsupervised learning.

00:17:19.620 --> 00:17:23.160
The key difference is that with
supervised learning,

00:17:23.160 --> 00:17:25.300
we have labeled data.

00:17:25.300 --> 00:17:27.700
Can you tell me what labeled
data is? I just mentioned it,

00:17:27.700 --> 00:17:29.340
but I want to hear it in your
own words.

00:17:29.340 --> 00:17:30.520
What is labeled data?

00:17:30.520 --> 00:17:30.980
Gavin.

00:17:30.980 --> 00:17:32.760
When we were doing the numbers,

00:17:32.760 --> 00:17:36.040
we categorized all the zeros
into one group and all the ones.

00:17:36.040 --> 00:17:38.420
We didn't just throw them all in
randomly.

00:17:38.420 --> 00:17:39.420
Exactly.
You want to add to that and

00:17:39.420 --> 00:17:40.460
remind us of your name?

00:17:40.460 --> 00:17:42.140
I'm Drew.
There's like a direct answer.

00:17:42.360 --> 00:17:42.900
Exactly.

00:17:42.900 --> 00:17:45.380
We know the right answer.

00:17:45.380 --> 00:17:48.280
So in label data,

00:17:48.280 --> 00:17:52.380
we know

00:17:52.380 --> 00:17:56.020
the right answer.

00:17:59.360 --> 00:18:01.880
So if we have all of the answers
already, why are we trying to

00:18:01.880 --> 00:18:05.630
predict anything in the first
place?

00:18:06.740 --> 00:18:08.760
What's the point?

00:18:08.760 --> 00:18:11.280
Predict stuff that's not in your
data set already.

00:18:11.280 --> 00:18:13.960
Exactly, the whole point is to
use this data set that we've

00:18:13.960 --> 00:18:16.840
collected to learn patterns so
that we can go and make

00:18:16.840 --> 00:18:19.840
predictions when we don't
already know the right answer.

00:18:19.840 --> 00:18:22.720
When I went to that handwritten
digit classification box and

00:18:22.720 --> 00:18:25.560
started drawing my own ones and
twos and threes,

00:18:25.560 --> 00:18:28.040
the people who made that system
30 years ago didn't know what I

00:18:28.040 --> 00:18:29.700
was going to draw.

00:18:29.700 --> 00:18:31.820
So they don't already know what
I was going to draw.

00:18:31.820 --> 00:18:35.160
What they tried to do is use the
data they had access to to build

00:18:35.160 --> 00:18:37.500
as accurate of a system as they
could.

00:18:37.500 --> 00:18:40.980
So that's ultimately the point
of building a machine learning

00:18:40.980 --> 00:18:41.540
model.

00:18:41.540 --> 00:18:44.840
You use the data that you've
collected

00:18:44.840 --> 00:18:48.060
to build a model that you think
will work well in the future

00:18:48.060 --> 00:18:51.460
when you don't already know the
right answer.

00:18:51.460 --> 00:18:56.600
So I like thinking of supervised
learning problems as sort of

00:18:56.600 --> 00:19:01.240
problems of the form predict y
given

00:19:01.240 --> 00:19:04.420
x, okay.

00:19:05.380 --> 00:19:07.840
That's a supervised learning
problem.

00:19:07.840 --> 00:19:10.840
Your data set has some x's and
y's and what you want to do is

00:19:10.840 --> 00:19:13.640
come up with a way to predict y
from x.

00:19:13.640 --> 00:19:18.180
Within supervised learning there
are two broad sort of subgroups.

00:19:20.620 --> 00:19:22.580
Regression and classification.

00:19:22.580 --> 00:19:24.340
Can someone tell me the
difference if you happen to have

00:19:24.340 --> 00:19:27.000
heard of these terms before.

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:29.680
What is regression and what is
classification?

00:19:29.680 --> 00:19:31.220
Yes, and remind us of your name?

00:19:31.220 --> 00:19:32.220
Charlie.

00:19:33.460 --> 00:19:37.480
Regression is to predict a value
based

00:19:37.480 --> 00:19:40.820
on the past patterns of the
data, and classification is to

00:19:40.820 --> 00:19:44.800
give a class of a data used to
do prediction.

00:19:44.800 --> 00:19:45.800
Exactly.
You're on the right track.

00:19:46.300 --> 00:19:48.550
Both regression and
classification

00:19:48.920 --> 00:19:52.640
are problems in which the form
is predict y from x.

00:19:52.640 --> 00:19:56.390
The difference is that with
regression,

00:19:57.040 --> 00:19:59.290
y is numerical,

00:20:00.120 --> 00:20:01.620
or continuous,

00:20:03.440 --> 00:20:05.700
versus in classification,

00:20:06.260 --> 00:20:09.260
y is a category.

00:20:11.180 --> 00:20:11.840
Okay,

00:20:11.840 --> 00:20:13.640
so that handwritten digit
example,

00:20:13.640 --> 00:20:16.640
was that regression or
classification?

00:20:18.060 --> 00:20:19.380
It's a little ambiguous.

00:20:19.380 --> 00:20:20.340
Yes, and remind us of your name?

00:20:20.340 --> 00:20:21.340
Alex.
Alex.

00:20:21.620 --> 00:20:22.460
It's classification.

00:20:22.460 --> 00:20:22.900
Why?

00:20:22.900 --> 00:20:26.300
Even though we were predicting
numbers.

00:20:26.300 --> 00:20:28.300
Because there are only 10 values
that can be.

00:20:28.300 --> 00:20:29.400
Yeah, and remind us of your
name?

00:20:29.400 --> 00:20:29.960
Gray.

00:20:29.960 --> 00:20:30.500
Gray.

00:20:30.500 --> 00:20:31.240
Yeah.

00:20:31.240 --> 00:20:34.020
So the handwritten digit example
is a little ambiguous, but it's

00:20:34.020 --> 00:20:36.340
clearly classification, even
though it seems like we're

00:20:36.340 --> 00:20:37.580
predicting numbers.

00:20:37.580 --> 00:20:40.400
And it's because those numbers
in that case were just symbols

00:20:40.400 --> 00:20:41.880
that we needed to predict.

00:20:41.880 --> 00:20:43.420
OK, they just happen to look
like numbers.

00:20:43.420 --> 00:20:46.420
But if instead I change the
problem to predictive this is an

00:20:46.420 --> 00:20:49.040
ABCDEFG or H.
It's the same thing

00:20:49.040 --> 00:20:50.940
mathematically The symbols just
look different.

00:20:50.940 --> 00:20:54.440
So it should be categorized the
same way in this taxonomy.

00:20:54.440 --> 00:20:55.440
Okay?

00:20:55.920 --> 00:20:59.420
So things like predicting a
handwritten digit or predicting

00:20:59.420 --> 00:21:01.680
true or false.
It's going to snow tomorrow or

00:21:02.460 --> 00:21:05.640
Predicting this is pictures of a
cat dog hamster or zebra.

00:21:05.640 --> 00:21:07.500
Those are all

00:21:07.500 --> 00:21:09.000
classification tasks

00:21:09.120 --> 00:21:13.260
Regression tasks are ones where
the variable we are predicting

00:21:13.260 --> 00:21:16.260
is some continuous numerical
variable,

00:21:16.360 --> 00:21:19.220
like predicting what grade you
are going to get on the final

00:21:19.220 --> 00:21:21.980
exam using your midterm one and
midterm two grades.

00:21:21.980 --> 00:21:24.080
That's a regression problem.

00:21:24.080 --> 00:21:26.940
Or say the running example we
are going to use this semester,

00:21:26.940 --> 00:21:29.800
and we will start with that at
the end of today's lecture, is

00:21:29.800 --> 00:21:33.140
predicting how long it's going
to take for you to get to work

00:21:33.140 --> 00:21:35.940
tomorrow given various factors
about your commute.

00:21:35.940 --> 00:21:39.690
Right there, we're predicting a
commute time in minutes, OK?

00:21:39.880 --> 00:21:41.420
So are we clear on the
difference between regression

00:21:41.420 --> 00:21:42.920
and classification?

00:21:44.140 --> 00:21:45.020
OK,

00:21:45.020 --> 00:21:49.940
most of our semester is going to
be spent here

00:21:49.940 --> 00:21:54.040
on regression problems.
But that's just because I've

00:21:54.040 --> 00:21:57.480
created this story that
introduces all of the linear

00:21:57.480 --> 00:22:00.940
algebra ideas we need so that it
helps us solve this regression

00:22:00.940 --> 00:22:02.660
problem.
But that foundation we build

00:22:02.660 --> 00:22:05.240
will equally apply to all of
these other branches of machine

00:22:05.240 --> 00:22:08.990
learning in lots of problems
that I haven't written on here.

00:22:10.480 --> 00:22:12.960
So we've talked about supervised
learning,

00:22:12.960 --> 00:22:15.540
and that's where we're going to
live for most of the semester.

00:22:15.540 --> 00:22:17.420
But at the very end of the
semester, we're going to touch

00:22:17.420 --> 00:22:19.460
on unsupervised learning.

00:22:19.460 --> 00:22:23.210
Now if supervised learning is
about predicting y from x,

00:22:23.280 --> 00:22:25.340
and we have a right answer,

00:22:25.340 --> 00:22:29.090
what do you think unsupervised
learning is?

00:22:29.960 --> 00:22:32.040
Yes, and write it in your name?

00:22:32.040 --> 00:22:34.100
Okay.
It's like you don't have a right

00:22:34.100 --> 00:22:35.520
answer.
Yeah, so what are you trying to

00:22:35.520 --> 00:22:36.840
do instead?

00:22:36.840 --> 00:22:41.340
Maybe do something and not have
anything to match it with.

00:22:42.540 --> 00:22:44.040
You're on the right track.

00:22:44.040 --> 00:22:45.420
The idea,

00:22:45.420 --> 00:22:48.100
exactly, is that we don't have a
right answer.

00:22:48.100 --> 00:22:51.140
What that means is our goal
isn't actually to predict

00:22:51.140 --> 00:22:51.780
anything.

00:22:51.780 --> 00:22:55.530
Instead, it's to find structure
within the data we have.

00:22:55.660 --> 00:22:59.120
Let me give you two examples of
what this type of problem may

00:22:59.120 --> 00:23:00.620
look like.

00:23:01.180 --> 00:23:04.980
So I wrote these course notes
last summer When the show The

00:23:04.980 --> 00:23:06.380
Summer I Turned Pretty was
popular,

00:23:06.380 --> 00:23:09.260
and that's what we were watching
at home and Grey's Anatomy

00:23:09.260 --> 00:23:11.040
Popular, so these are the shows
we were watching at home.

00:23:11.040 --> 00:23:12.760
So these are the examples I used

00:23:12.760 --> 00:23:15.000
Pretend I run Netflix or Hulu

00:23:15.000 --> 00:23:17.340
There's no streaming service
that has both of these shows on

00:23:17.340 --> 00:23:20.980
but pretend one of them did and
for each of my users

00:23:20.980 --> 00:23:25.080
I kept track of how much they
like each of the shows they're

00:23:25.080 --> 00:23:25.780
watching

00:23:25.780 --> 00:23:26.640
Okay,

00:23:26.640 --> 00:23:29.720
so I have some hypothetical
group of say nine users here and

00:23:29.720 --> 00:23:32.900
for each one I know how much
they liked The Samurai Turned

00:23:32.900 --> 00:23:36.060
Pretty and how much they liked
Grey's Anatomy.

00:23:36.060 --> 00:23:38.700
Now that's all I have.
I just have these ratings.

00:23:38.700 --> 00:23:41.060
But if I plot this out,

00:23:41.060 --> 00:23:43.760
what does- what are you seeing
here?

00:23:43.760 --> 00:23:45.360
What are you seeing?

00:23:45.360 --> 00:23:47.260
Yes, and- Greetings?

00:23:47.260 --> 00:23:48.260
Connor.

00:23:48.580 --> 00:23:51.180
It kind of goes in a little
squiggle, so like if you like

00:23:51.180 --> 00:23:53.680
The Samurai Turned Pretty you
don't necessarily like Grey's

00:23:53.680 --> 00:23:57.460
Anatomy as much or you also
really like it or you like both

00:23:57.460 --> 00:24:00.360
of them kind of like a fair
amount.

00:24:00.360 --> 00:24:01.000
Yeah,

00:24:01.000 --> 00:24:05.500
it seems like, and I think this
may be what you were getting at,

00:24:05.500 --> 00:24:09.980
it seems like roughly the users,

00:24:09.980 --> 00:24:12.740
even though we didn't already
have them placed into groups,

00:24:12.740 --> 00:24:15.660
all they did was just make a
Netflix account and watch shows,

00:24:15.660 --> 00:24:19.280
it seems like they naturally
fall into some of these groups.

00:24:19.280 --> 00:24:22.280
These groups are called
clusters.

00:24:23.620 --> 00:24:25.980
And clustering is an example

00:24:25.980 --> 00:24:31.760
of an unsupervised learning

00:24:31.760 --> 00:24:35.320
problem, where you have a bunch
of data points in high

00:24:35.320 --> 00:24:36.320
dimensions.

00:24:36.580 --> 00:24:40.260
And your job is to place them
into groups where users or data

00:24:40.260 --> 00:24:43.400
points in each group are similar
to one another and different

00:24:43.400 --> 00:24:45.040
from the other groups.

00:24:45.040 --> 00:24:47.400
Why might you want to do this?

00:24:47.400 --> 00:24:49.620
Again, pretend you're running
the streaming service And you

00:24:49.620 --> 00:24:51.300
have a bunch of users,

00:24:51.300 --> 00:24:53.540
and you place them into groups.

00:24:53.540 --> 00:24:54.640
Why might you want to do that?

00:24:54.640 --> 00:24:55.900
Yeah, and what was your name?

00:24:55.900 --> 00:24:56.520
Alessandra.

00:24:56.520 --> 00:24:58.460
Alessandra.
You could recommend them shows

00:24:58.460 --> 00:25:00.100
based off the cluster.

00:25:00.100 --> 00:25:02.160
Exactly.
What you could do is recommend

00:25:02.160 --> 00:25:05.920
all of the users in a cluster
the same types of shows.

00:25:05.920 --> 00:25:07.460
So if these two users,

00:25:07.460 --> 00:25:10.040
the ones at 8, 3 and 10, 4,

00:25:10.040 --> 00:25:13.740
if they both like some new show,

00:25:13.740 --> 00:25:15.960
what were we watching yesterday,
One Tree Hill.

00:25:15.960 --> 00:25:17.820
If they both like One Tree Hill,
well,

00:25:17.820 --> 00:25:20.960
then maybe this person will also
like that, because they all have

00:25:20.960 --> 00:25:22.040
similar tastes.

00:25:22.040 --> 00:25:23.880
But maybe people in other
clusters,

00:25:23.880 --> 00:25:26.540
I don't have enough information
to recommend them One Tree Hill

00:25:26.540 --> 00:25:27.580
as well,

00:25:27.580 --> 00:25:30.740
because we don't know that they
like the same things that the

00:25:30.740 --> 00:25:33.860
people in the bottom right
cluster like.

00:25:33.860 --> 00:25:36.120
And so now when you go on a
streaming service, Netflix,

00:25:36.120 --> 00:25:37.000
Hulu, whatever,

00:25:37.000 --> 00:25:41.740
you see how they start to come
up with recommendations for you.

00:25:41.740 --> 00:25:45.420
So that's one example of an
unsupervised learning problem.

00:25:45.420 --> 00:25:47.360
Now, in practice,

00:25:47.360 --> 00:25:50.940
figuring out what the groups are
is much more difficult than just

00:25:50.940 --> 00:25:52.600
eyeballing it.
Why is that?

00:25:53.160 --> 00:25:56.140
Well, it's because these
streaming services don't just

00:25:56.140 --> 00:25:59.300
keep track of how much you like
two different shows.

00:25:59.300 --> 00:26:02.160
They keep track of how much you
like every show you've watched,

00:26:02.160 --> 00:26:03.480
and that may be hundreds.

00:26:03.480 --> 00:26:05.540
And you factor in all the movies
as well.

00:26:05.540 --> 00:26:08.360
So instead of being able to draw
a graph in two dimensions,

00:26:08.360 --> 00:26:11.680
we're thinking in tens or
hundreds of dimensions.

00:26:11.680 --> 00:26:15.780
Okay, and then the question is,
how do we still place users into

00:26:15.780 --> 00:26:18.840
groups when we have so many
pieces of information for each

00:26:18.840 --> 00:26:21.360
one and we can't just eyeball
the groups ourselves?

00:26:21.360 --> 00:26:23.940
That's the kind of problem
machine learning will solve,

00:26:23.940 --> 00:26:28.440
and this sort of thinking of
data points in high dimensions,

00:26:28.720 --> 00:26:32.640
dimensions higher than 2D and
3D, is the type of skill

00:26:33.320 --> 00:26:37.220
that we're going to develop in
this class.

