00:27:07.220 --> 00:27:11.240
Another example of a machine
learning problem is one of

00:27:11.240 --> 00:27:12.740
dimensionality reduction.

00:27:14.140 --> 00:27:15.740
What does this mean?

00:27:15.740 --> 00:27:19.320
And I'll show you the picture I
wanted to show you in the notes.

00:27:19.320 --> 00:27:24.700
Let's go back to that example
down

00:27:24.700 --> 00:27:29.780
here or just work with us for a
second.

00:27:29.780 --> 00:27:33.820
And we just said in this
hypothetical streaming service,

00:27:33.820 --> 00:27:38.320
we have scores for the various
shows that a user has watched.

00:27:38.740 --> 00:27:42.080
And if there are lots of shows
that each user has watched,

00:27:42.080 --> 00:27:44.700
then it's impossible to
visualize all of the data at

00:27:44.700 --> 00:27:45.600
once.

00:27:45.600 --> 00:27:48.660
Because if you want one axis per
show,

00:27:48.660 --> 00:27:51.080
well then if they've watched any
more than three shows,

00:27:51.080 --> 00:27:53.180
we can't visualize this data.

00:27:53.180 --> 00:27:58.200
Dimensionality reduction is a
tool for taking high-dimensional

00:27:58.200 --> 00:27:59.200
data

00:27:59.840 --> 00:28:04.760
and turning it into a
lower-dimensional representation

00:28:04.760 --> 00:28:07.300
that we can visualize in two or
three dimensions.

00:28:07.300 --> 00:28:12.580
So imagine I have a table in
which there are n rows,

00:28:13.020 --> 00:28:15.270
one per user,

00:28:16.660 --> 00:28:21.200
and d columns,

00:28:22.560 --> 00:28:24.810
one per show.

00:28:25.740 --> 00:28:27.260
And just as an example,

00:28:27.260 --> 00:28:30.000
let's just say we have a
thousand columns.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:33.880
So for example, this first
column could be the summer I

00:28:33.880 --> 00:28:35.780
turn pretty column.

00:28:35.780 --> 00:28:39.240
The next could be the Grey's
Anatomy column.

00:28:39.240 --> 00:28:41.680
The next could be the One Tree
Hill column, and so on and so

00:28:41.680 --> 00:28:42.680
forth.

00:28:42.700 --> 00:28:44.680
You can't visualize this data.

00:28:44.680 --> 00:28:47.680
But through dimensionality
reduction techniques,

00:28:49.420 --> 00:28:55.380
what you can produce

00:28:55.380 --> 00:28:57.740
is a smaller dataset

00:28:57.740 --> 00:29:00.720
with still one row per user,
because you want to keep the

00:29:00.720 --> 00:29:02.720
information you have for every
user,

00:29:02.720 --> 00:29:06.740
but instead of needing 1,000
pieces of information for each

00:29:06.740 --> 00:29:07.480
user,

00:29:07.480 --> 00:29:11.300
you can summarize or reduce that
information into just two or

00:29:11.300 --> 00:29:13.920
three pieces of information per
user.

00:29:13.920 --> 00:29:16.720
Now, the naive way to do that
would be to just pick the shows

00:29:16.720 --> 00:29:18.540
that you thought were most
important.

00:29:18.540 --> 00:29:19.600
And you could do that.

00:29:19.600 --> 00:29:22.100
But there's actually an even
more informative way of doing

00:29:22.100 --> 00:29:27.420
that, which involves creating
new sort of hypothetical TV

00:29:27.420 --> 00:29:30.400
shows that you create using a
combination of each of the

00:29:30.400 --> 00:29:31.800
original TV shows.

00:29:31.800 --> 00:29:34.360
And when you create these new
hypothetical TV shows,

00:29:34.360 --> 00:29:40.080
you do that in a way that sort
of keeps and retains as

00:29:40.080 --> 00:29:43.830
much of the variance between
users as possible.

00:29:46.760 --> 00:29:51.360
And so these new columns I might
call new feature

00:29:51.360 --> 00:29:53.060
one

00:29:53.420 --> 00:29:57.300
and new feature two.

