00:30:28.880 --> 00:30:34.130
What we will learn to do is
think of an image as a matrix.

00:30:34.620 --> 00:30:38.960
You can think of it as a table
with rows and columns.

00:30:38.960 --> 00:30:41.680
And each pixel in the image,

00:30:41.680 --> 00:30:45.400
in this example, this image is
grayscale.

00:30:45.400 --> 00:30:48.540
We can just represent each pixel
with just a number from 0 to

00:30:48.540 --> 00:30:49.540
255.

00:30:49.680 --> 00:30:52.080
That's a standard way that
colors are represented.

00:30:52.080 --> 00:30:56.040
0 means the pixel is dark or
black here, and 255 would mean

00:30:56.040 --> 00:30:58.080
that it's white.

00:30:58.080 --> 00:31:00.300
To store this image,

00:31:00.300 --> 00:31:04.500
I believe it's a 300 by 400
image, so 300 pixels wide, 400

00:31:04.500 --> 00:31:05.900
pixels tall,

00:31:05.900 --> 00:31:10.660
I would need to store 1.2
million numbers,

00:31:11.860 --> 00:31:17.110
like the pixel amount at each of
these 400 by 300 pixels.

00:31:17.260 --> 00:31:22.400
Linear algebra is going to give
us a way to compress the image

00:31:22.400 --> 00:31:23.400
optimally.

00:31:25.340 --> 00:31:27.640
And so what you're seeing here,
and if you go to chapter 10.2

00:31:27.640 --> 00:31:30.180
in the notes, you'll see a
slider for this.

00:31:30.180 --> 00:31:34.520
What we've visualized is what's
called a rank k approximation of

00:31:34.520 --> 00:31:35.980
the image.

00:31:35.980 --> 00:31:39.340
And you can think of, for now,
the rank of the matrix as being

00:31:39.340 --> 00:31:41.860
the amount of information in it.

00:31:41.860 --> 00:31:44.780
And right now, the slider is at
k is equal to 1.

00:31:44.780 --> 00:31:47.620
And what do you notice about
this low rank approximation of

00:31:47.620 --> 00:31:49.120
the image?

00:31:49.460 --> 00:31:51.180
It's not a dog,

00:31:51.180 --> 00:31:52.920
it's not really much of
anything.

00:31:52.920 --> 00:31:55.080
And what you should actually
notice here is that every column

00:31:55.080 --> 00:31:58.680
of this image is really just the
same column, some are brighter

00:31:58.680 --> 00:32:00.280
than others.
But there's really only one

00:32:00.280 --> 00:32:02.720
pattern, you see this like big
cross here.

00:32:02.720 --> 00:32:07.460
But as I add to the value of K,

00:32:07.460 --> 00:32:12.360
you can see slowly the picture
of Junior starts to get

00:32:12.360 --> 00:32:13.360
reproduced,

00:32:14.100 --> 00:32:16.940
okay? And in order to reproduce
the full picture,

00:32:16.940 --> 00:32:21.220
you would need the full rank of
the matrix, which here would be

00:32:21.220 --> 00:32:22.720
likely 300.

00:32:23.360 --> 00:32:26.220
But what we're seeing is that at
rank 30, if you squint, it does

00:32:26.220 --> 00:32:28.620
still look like junior.

00:32:28.620 --> 00:32:32.000
And it turns out you can store
these low rank

00:32:32.000 --> 00:32:34.980
approximations very efficiently
using linear algebra.

