01:04:07.160 --> 01:04:11.660
here's a function that could do
that.

01:04:19.140 --> 01:04:22.060
Why is this bad?

01:04:22.060 --> 01:04:27.310
It is what we call overfit to
the training data.

01:04:36.900 --> 01:04:41.200
And the reason that that's bad
is that it has clearly gone

01:04:41.200 --> 01:04:45.460
beyond the general trend in the
relationship between commute

01:04:45.460 --> 01:04:48.780
time and departure time, and it
has all of these little wiggles

01:04:48.780 --> 01:04:53.840
and bumps which are unlikely to
represent a broader trend.

01:04:55.440 --> 01:04:59.600
And so what this means is if I
go and try and use this orange

01:04:59.600 --> 01:05:02.440
curve to predict how long
tomorrow's commute is going to

01:05:02.440 --> 01:05:05.820
take and how I would use it is
go to wherever I plan on leaving

01:05:05.820 --> 01:05:09.020
in the morning on the x-axis and
look at the height of this graph

01:05:09.020 --> 01:05:10.680
on the y-axis.

01:05:10.680 --> 01:05:14.280
This would do a worse job than
far simpler models would,

01:05:14.280 --> 01:05:18.360
because this has way too many
squiggles that don't represent a

01:05:18.360 --> 01:05:20.260
general trend.
They just represent the specific

01:05:20.260 --> 01:05:23.280
traffic on any one day or the
fact that I stopped at Starbucks

01:05:23.280 --> 01:05:27.030
coming home or something like
that.

01:05:34.280 --> 01:05:36.460
And so keep this at the back of
your mind.

01:05:36.460 --> 01:05:41.710
Our goal is to build models that
generalize well to unseen data.

01:05:48.560 --> 01:05:50.810
Now with that,

01:05:51.120 --> 01:05:53.640
I've used the word model,

01:05:53.640 --> 01:05:57.390
but I haven't actually defined
what I mean by a model.

01:05:59.380 --> 01:06:00.260
A model,

01:06:00.260 --> 01:06:02.300
you know, people use this word a
lot and you say, okay,

01:06:02.300 --> 01:06:05.280
chat jibbity the new 5.6
model or whatever,

01:06:05.280 --> 01:06:09.260
right? One unifying term that
defines all types of models,

01:06:09.420 --> 01:06:10.220
statistical,

01:06:10.220 --> 01:06:12.100
large language model, machine
learning,

01:06:12.100 --> 01:06:18.100
is that a model is a set of
assumptions

01:06:23.280 --> 01:06:28.980
about how data was generated,

01:06:30.160 --> 01:06:34.420
or equivalently,

01:06:38.240 --> 01:06:41.240
how the world works.

01:06:42.160 --> 01:06:45.720
If you think philosophically for
a moment any model you've heard

01:06:45.720 --> 01:06:48.960
of is just an assumption about
how one specific part of the

01:06:48.960 --> 01:06:50.460
world works.

01:06:50.860 --> 01:06:54.280
Now there's a famous quote that
you may have heard of in like a

01:06:54.280 --> 01:06:58.240
high school statistics or
science class which is that all

01:06:58.240 --> 01:07:01.260
models are wrong but some are
useful.

01:07:01.260 --> 01:07:02.940
Has anyone heard this before?

01:07:02.940 --> 01:07:04.040
A few people.

01:07:04.040 --> 01:07:05.600
What is the essence of that
statement?

01:07:05.600 --> 01:07:07.440
All models are wrong, but some
are useful.

01:07:07.440 --> 01:07:11.040
Like, what does that mean?

01:07:11.040 --> 01:07:12.220
Yeah.
And remind us of your name?

01:07:12.740 --> 01:07:13.740
Zara.

01:07:14.080 --> 01:07:15.300
Maybe it's like,

01:07:15.300 --> 01:07:20.550
not every model can be right all
the time.

01:07:21.720 --> 01:07:22.720
Exactly.

01:07:22.760 --> 01:07:27.820
What this is saying is that no
matter how hard you try,

01:07:27.820 --> 01:07:31.160
models will make mistakes.
They won't reflect every single

01:07:31.160 --> 01:07:34.040
part of how the world works.
And we see that even now when we

01:07:34.040 --> 01:07:37.220
have these billion or trillion
dollar models released by the

01:07:37.220 --> 01:07:39.260
big AI companies that still
don't answer everything

01:07:39.260 --> 01:07:40.260
correctly.

01:07:41.260 --> 01:07:43.140
And so one interpretation of
this,

01:07:43.140 --> 01:07:43.700
and I'm sorry,

01:07:43.700 --> 01:07:46.100
all models are wrong, but some
are useful.

01:07:46.100 --> 01:07:47.460
What does that mean?

01:07:47.460 --> 01:07:49.980
Well, it means that even simple
models

01:07:49.980 --> 01:07:52.920
can explain useful things about
how the world works.

01:07:52.920 --> 01:07:56.140
The example I like using for
this is the fact you learned in

01:07:56.140 --> 01:08:00.000
high school science or physics,
which is that acceleration due

01:08:00.000 --> 01:08:03.300
to gravity is 9.81
meters per second squared.

01:08:03.740 --> 01:08:05.200
Do we remember this fact?

01:08:05.200 --> 01:08:06.620
It's not always true,

01:08:06.620 --> 01:08:08.500
right? If you drop, I don't
know, if I drop a piece of

01:08:08.500 --> 01:08:10.540
paper, that's not the speed at
which it's going to accelerate,

01:08:10.540 --> 01:08:12.640
right? There are other variables
that affect this.

01:08:12.640 --> 01:08:15.000
It affects, you know, things
like how far you are from the

01:08:15.000 --> 01:08:17.340
equator and what the drag
coefficient of the thing you

01:08:17.340 --> 01:08:18.420
dropped is.

01:08:18.420 --> 01:08:22.020
Still, that simple model is
useful enough for us to be able

01:08:22.020 --> 01:08:24.270
to do calculations,

01:08:24.480 --> 01:08:27.960
okay? And so what I'm getting at
here is that even the most

01:08:27.960 --> 01:08:30.460
complicated models will be
wrong.

01:08:30.460 --> 01:08:34.480
And so there actually is some
utility in studying simpler

01:08:34.480 --> 01:08:35.480
models.

01:08:36.280 --> 01:08:39.580
And so what we are going to do
in this class

01:08:39.580 --> 01:08:44.060
is actually start

01:08:44.060 --> 01:08:48.580
with models that seem overly
simplistic.

01:08:49.300 --> 01:08:50.760
When you look at this,

01:08:50.760 --> 01:08:54.480
what was the first type of model
that you assumed we would draw

01:08:54.480 --> 01:08:55.880
on here,

01:08:55.880 --> 01:08:57.780
knowing nothing else.

01:08:57.780 --> 01:08:58.140
Yeah,

01:08:58.140 --> 01:08:59.360
Paul? Like linear regression?

01:08:59.360 --> 01:09:02.560
Yeah, what you saw here is maybe
something you saw in a high

01:09:02.560 --> 01:09:04.810
school statistics class,

01:09:05.060 --> 01:09:08.740
something like a line of best
fit

01:09:08.740 --> 01:09:13.960
that we could use to predict
commute time given departure

01:09:13.960 --> 01:09:14.960
time.

01:09:15.620 --> 01:09:18.080
And indeed, this is a valid
model, and it's one that we will

01:09:18.080 --> 01:09:20.060
study.
Here, we will call that the

01:09:20.060 --> 01:09:23.820
simple linear

01:09:23.820 --> 01:09:27.960
regression model.

01:09:29.760 --> 01:09:34.260
Simple just means that it only
has one input variable.

01:09:36.340 --> 01:09:40.180
And again, input variables are
what we call features.

01:09:41.520 --> 01:09:44.900
Here, our only feature is
departure time.

01:09:44.900 --> 01:09:48.520
So yes, this is a model we will
study.

01:09:48.520 --> 01:09:53.020
But I'm actually going to start
even simpler than that.

01:09:53.500 --> 01:09:56.800
What model could be simpler than
this?

01:09:56.800 --> 01:09:58.840
How could we be even more basic?

01:09:58.840 --> 01:10:00.540
Yes, and remind us of your name?
I'm Alex.

01:10:00.540 --> 01:10:02.360
Alex.
You have a constant model? A

01:10:02.360 --> 01:10:04.540
constant model, good.

01:10:04.540 --> 01:10:07.640
Here, this model returns
different predictions depending

01:10:07.640 --> 01:10:09.660
the time we leave in the
morning.

01:10:09.660 --> 01:10:13.240
What if I say, no, that's too
complicated?

01:10:13.240 --> 01:10:17.740
why don't I instead just always
predict the same commute time?

01:10:22.960 --> 01:10:24.460
Sounds basic,

01:10:24.600 --> 01:10:27.460
and indeed the simple linear
regression model here is better

01:10:27.460 --> 01:10:30.260
in almost every way.
It'll also generalize just as

01:10:30.260 --> 01:10:33.380
well, but the constant model is
not totally useless because

01:10:33.380 --> 01:10:36.400
imagine you had a conversation
with someone about, you know,

01:10:36.400 --> 01:10:38.380
you have this historical data
about your commutes and they

01:10:38.380 --> 01:10:39.020
ask,

01:10:39.020 --> 01:10:42.380
okay how long does it usually
take for you to get to work,

01:10:42.380 --> 01:10:44.500
you're probably not going to
give them a complicated formula.

01:10:44.500 --> 01:10:46.260
You'll give them a one-number
summary.

01:10:46.260 --> 01:10:49.040
And that's really just the
constant model.

01:10:49.040 --> 01:10:52.020
And so here's the philosophy
we're going to use here.

01:10:52.020 --> 01:10:54.440
I want to start with the
constant model.

01:10:54.440 --> 01:10:55.480
So today,

01:10:55.480 --> 01:10:56.080
Thursday,

01:10:56.080 --> 01:10:59.160
next Tuesday, we're mostly just
going to talk about the constant

01:10:59.160 --> 01:10:59.800
model.

01:10:59.800 --> 01:11:03.080
But I'm going to use it to
introduce a lot of notation

01:11:03.080 --> 01:11:08.500
formulas and machinery that we
will use throughout our machine

01:11:08.500 --> 01:11:10.060
learning journey.
So we're going to start with a

01:11:10.060 --> 01:11:11.560
simple model,

01:11:11.620 --> 01:11:15.360
develop what I call the
three-step modeling recipe,

01:11:15.360 --> 01:11:18.760
and then throughout the
semester, look at more and more

01:11:18.760 --> 01:11:21.760
complicated models through that
same recipe.

01:11:21.860 --> 01:11:24.340
So we'll start basic and then
increase our complexity from

01:11:24.340 --> 01:11:25.340
there.

01:11:29.600 --> 01:11:33.580
Now, let's introduce a tiny bit
of notation here.

01:11:33.580 --> 01:11:37.120
A hypothesis function

01:11:37.120 --> 01:11:42.800
is a

01:11:42.800 --> 01:11:48.700
function which I'll denote with
the letter H takes in features

01:11:53.240 --> 01:11:59.240
and returns predicted

01:11:59.240 --> 01:12:01.080
Y values.

01:12:02.580 --> 01:12:06.960
So, a hypothesis function is
what you use to make your

01:12:06.960 --> 01:12:10.060
predictions, and ultimately what
we are trying to do here is find

01:12:10.060 --> 01:12:12.360
good hypothesis functions,

01:12:14.480 --> 01:12:19.720
or build a useful

01:12:19.720 --> 01:12:23.260
hypothesis function.

01:12:29.120 --> 01:12:33.620
The idea is that each model,

01:12:34.280 --> 01:12:37.280
or each model type,

01:12:39.720 --> 01:12:45.340
so for example constant or
simple linear regression

01:12:45.340 --> 01:12:49.040
has its own

01:12:49.040 --> 01:12:55.020
hypothesis class format.

01:12:57.220 --> 01:13:01.960
So let me

01:13:01.960 --> 01:13:06.040
start with the constant model.

01:13:17.160 --> 01:13:23.120
The constant model is always

01:13:23.120 --> 01:13:28.980
of the form h of xi equals some
constant.

01:13:28.980 --> 01:13:31.660
I'll call it W.

01:13:31.660 --> 01:13:33.720
And what I'm seeing on the
right, I'll zoom in a little

01:13:33.720 --> 01:13:35.060
because I know this is small,

01:13:35.060 --> 01:13:38.880
is for three different values of
W,

01:13:38.880 --> 01:13:39.420
60,

01:13:39.420 --> 01:13:41.080
70, and 100,

01:13:41.080 --> 01:13:46.780
what the resulting constant
model looks like on our data

01:13:46.780 --> 01:13:47.780
set.

01:13:52.100 --> 01:13:55.420
Now, what you should see is that
some choices of w are better

01:13:55.420 --> 01:13:56.660
than others.

01:13:56.660 --> 01:14:01.260
Like, choosing w equals 100
seems a little nonsensical for

01:14:01.260 --> 01:14:04.680
this data set, because none of
the data,

01:14:04.680 --> 01:14:09.120
like very few of our commutes
were in the range of 100

01:14:09.120 --> 01:14:10.120
minutes.

01:14:11.300 --> 01:14:15.660
W equals 60 also seems to be a
little low,

01:14:15.660 --> 01:14:19.180
but the question is where do we
put it in the middle?

01:14:19.180 --> 01:14:20.780
Like 70 seems good.

01:14:20.780 --> 01:14:24.620
Do we agree that W equals 70
seems the best of these choices?

01:14:24.620 --> 01:14:25.500
Yeah,

01:14:25.500 --> 01:14:28.340
but maybe 71 is better, and
maybe 70.37

01:14:28.340 --> 01:14:30.060
is even better than that.

01:14:30.060 --> 01:14:31.700
How do we know?

01:14:31.700 --> 01:14:34.480
Okay, and that is the big
problem we are going to study on

01:14:34.480 --> 01:14:36.740
Thursday.
how do we figure out what to

01:14:36.740 --> 01:14:38.240
make W?

01:14:39.460 --> 01:14:44.710
W is what is called a parameter.

01:14:51.840 --> 01:14:57.200
A parameter is an aspect of a
model that we have to pick.

01:14:58.660 --> 01:15:01.660
So different parameters will
lead into different predictions.

01:15:02.280 --> 01:15:07.420
And w is always the letter we
will use for parameters.

01:15:07.420 --> 01:15:11.300
The constant model has just one
parameter.

01:15:11.300 --> 01:15:14.780
Yes? What is a parameter
different from a feature?

01:15:14.780 --> 01:15:17.440
A feature is the input variable.

01:15:17.440 --> 01:15:18.940
So like,

01:15:19.640 --> 01:15:21.940
here, let me give you an example
of the simple linear regression

01:15:21.940 --> 01:15:23.520
model.
And I think that'll make it a

01:15:23.520 --> 01:15:24.640
little more clear.

01:15:24.640 --> 01:15:26.400
We'll come back to the constant
model in a second.

01:15:26.400 --> 01:15:30.150
And the simple linear regression
model,

01:15:39.060 --> 01:15:43.560
various versions of it look like
this.

01:15:44.000 --> 01:15:48.500
How many parameters do you think
it has?

01:15:52.840 --> 01:15:55.280
How many parameters do you think
the simple linear regression

01:15:55.280 --> 01:15:57.920
model has? Yes, and remind us of
your name.

01:15:57.920 --> 01:15:58.320
Oh,

01:15:58.320 --> 01:15:58.720
sorry.

01:15:58.720 --> 01:15:59.080
Yeah,

01:15:59.080 --> 01:16:00.180
I'll admit it, but yeah.

01:16:00.180 --> 01:16:01.180
Sam.
Sam, yeah.

01:16:01.480 --> 01:16:02.000
Two.

01:16:02.000 --> 01:16:02.740
Two.

01:16:02.740 --> 01:16:06.420
And what are, OK, what are those
parameters?

01:16:06.420 --> 01:16:08.260
Slope and the wider set.
Good.

01:16:08.260 --> 01:16:09.280
And what was your name?

01:16:09.280 --> 01:16:10.460
Chinmay.
Chinmay, awesome.

01:16:11.460 --> 01:16:12.280
Good.

01:16:12.280 --> 01:16:15.280
The simple linear regression
model has two parameters,

01:16:16.360 --> 01:16:20.860
two things we can tweak about
the model.

01:16:33.460 --> 01:16:34.660
The X's,

01:16:34.660 --> 01:16:37.080
those are the features.

01:16:37.080 --> 01:16:39.960
Orange is a bad color for that.

01:16:39.960 --> 01:16:43.710
I'll just use blue here.

01:16:48.720 --> 01:16:52.280
So remember, before I said the
X's represent the commute,

01:16:52.280 --> 01:16:55.260
sorry, the departure times, and
the y's represent the commute

01:16:55.260 --> 01:16:56.100
times.

01:16:56.100 --> 01:16:58.380
So xi, you should think of that
as like 8.30

01:16:58.380 --> 01:17:00.630
AM or 9.45
AM.

01:17:01.400 --> 01:17:05.900
The parameters are what we are
using

01:17:06.280 --> 01:17:08.440
to help us make the prediction.

01:17:08.440 --> 01:17:11.180
And in this case, what we do is
we take the intercept parameter,

01:17:11.180 --> 01:17:12.180
w0,

01:17:12.280 --> 01:17:16.380
add that to the slope parameter
multiplied by the feature.

01:17:16.380 --> 01:17:17.960
So is that distinction clear?

01:17:17.960 --> 01:17:18.300
OK,

01:17:18.300 --> 01:17:20.560
good question, because that's an
important thing to clarify.

01:17:20.560 --> 01:17:23.620
So the parameters are these w's
that we about the model,

01:17:23.620 --> 01:17:27.560
the X's are the input
information that we have from

01:17:27.560 --> 01:17:28.980
raw data.

01:17:28.980 --> 01:17:30.960
Are we clear on the difference
between a parameter and a

01:17:30.960 --> 01:17:31.960
feature?

01:17:32.040 --> 01:17:34.780
And we will cover this again on
Thursday because I want to make

01:17:34.780 --> 01:17:37.340
sure the notation is clear.

01:17:37.340 --> 01:17:40.280
Now this definition of a
parameter is the same as the

01:17:40.280 --> 01:17:43.560
definition of a parameter you
hear about in large language

01:17:43.560 --> 01:17:44.460
models.

01:17:44.460 --> 01:17:46.860
But this model just has two
parameters.

01:17:46.860 --> 01:17:49.960
The latest, Chachipiti and
Clods, have hundreds of billions

01:17:49.960 --> 01:17:51.460
of parameters.

01:17:52.780 --> 01:17:58.020
And so they can perform more
sophisticated calculations and

01:17:58.020 --> 01:18:01.820
be used to predict more
complicated things than just the

01:18:01.820 --> 01:18:03.120
commute time tomorrow.

01:18:03.120 --> 01:18:05.100
But it's the same essence.

01:18:05.100 --> 01:18:08.800
And to train those models, all
they are doing is figuring out

01:18:08.800 --> 01:18:11.820
what the best w's to use are.
It's the same principle that we

01:18:11.820 --> 01:18:14.070
are using here.

01:18:14.160 --> 01:18:15.160
OK?

01:18:16.300 --> 01:18:19.060
Here, for the simple linear
regression model,

01:18:19.060 --> 01:18:22.220
I've shown three different sets
of parameters.

01:18:22.220 --> 01:18:25.460
In the first I used an intercept
of 160 and a slope of negative

01:18:25.460 --> 01:18:26.260
10,

01:18:26.260 --> 01:18:29.820
then I used an intercept of 20
and a slope of 5, and in the

01:18:29.820 --> 01:18:34.020
third I used an intercept of 100
and a slope of negative 3.

01:18:34.020 --> 01:18:37.120
And we see that some of these
choices are better than others.

01:18:37.120 --> 01:18:39.820
The first combination of
intercept and slope seems to be

01:18:39.820 --> 01:18:40.780
the best.

01:18:40.780 --> 01:18:43.720
The middle one seems to make no
sense at all.

01:18:43.720 --> 01:18:47.480
And the last one seems to be OK,
but worse than the first.

01:18:47.480 --> 01:18:48.980
We agree?

01:18:49.040 --> 01:18:50.500
OK, so the question is,

01:18:50.500 --> 01:18:55.000
what is the optimal or best
combination of parameters?

01:19:08.940 --> 01:19:13.440
It's the same question that I
posed with the constant model.

01:19:13.980 --> 01:19:17.540
We have the model drawn for
three different choices of the

01:19:17.540 --> 01:19:18.620
one parameter,

01:19:18.620 --> 01:19:19.620
W.

01:19:21.620 --> 01:19:24.420
For the are you drawing this
flat line,

01:19:24.420 --> 01:19:26.460
right? The constant model
doesn't have a slope.

01:19:26.460 --> 01:19:28.420
So the question is,

01:19:28.420 --> 01:19:32.920
what is the best w?

01:19:35.220 --> 01:19:37.340
Now, do you have some guesses?
In the context of the constant

01:19:37.340 --> 01:19:38.340
model,

01:19:38.980 --> 01:19:42.680
do you have guesses for what the
best w could be?

01:19:42.680 --> 01:19:44.320
The best constant to predict?

01:19:44.320 --> 01:19:45.600
Yes, and remind us of your name?

01:19:45.600 --> 01:19:46.140
Caitlin.

01:19:46.140 --> 01:19:48.980
Caitlin.
Is it like where you're the

01:19:48.980 --> 01:19:52.880
closest to most amount data
points you know? Yeah, it feels

01:19:52.880 --> 01:19:56.260
like the best W would be one
that seems to be like in the

01:19:56.260 --> 01:19:57.940
middle of the data somehow

01:19:57.940 --> 01:20:00.480
But there are lots of ways we
could formulate that like do we

